    {
      "card_ids": ["C09"],
      "claim": "Cyproconazole, a structurally analogous triazole antifungal with a tertiary alcohol and halophenyl group, was detected in tadpole brain tissue, demonstrating that this azole class can cross the blood-brain barrier.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Flumazenil, despite being a known P-gp substrate, readily entered the human brain with 9.3% whole-brain uptake, showing that efflux liability does not preclude meaningful CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "A CNS-penetrant P2X7 antagonist with a brain-to-plasma ratio of 0.71 was classified as having good BBB penetration, supporting that moderate B/P ratios can still represent adequate CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "A heteroaromatic compound with a Kp,uu near unity demonstrated good CNS penetration in rat pharmacokinetic studies, supporting that similar moderately polar, aromatic molecules can achieve meaningful brain exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query molecule is predicted to have meaningful CNS access. The closest structural analogue, cyproconazole, crosses the BBB, and multiple supportive CNS-penetration measurements from chemically relevant molecules reinforce this conclusion. No contradictory evidence was identified, and the meningitis condition would likely further enhance BBB permeability.",
